{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792269256,"nonce":"8M/Q9Q4dpsKuMTe+rCEtl601CA8=","title":"The code editor is the last widget built through a stateful callback, and its module is 3,000 lines","message":"`CodeEditor` (widgets/code/mod.rs) is a `Stateful\u003cTextFieldState\u003e` whose `state_callback` rebuilds the whole editor content (highlighted lines, gutter, selection, folds, search highlights, cursor) on every edit, click and hover. It is the one widget in blinc_layout/src/widgets and blinc_cn still built that way (text_input, text_area, rich_text_editor, number_input and input_otp were moved; the earlier count of \"no .on_state( left\" missed it because it assigns `state_callback` directly). Its find bar needed a refresh handle just to redraw it (`CodeEditorData::editor_stateful`).\n\nSame shape as the text widgets: a revision signal, lines as a keyed list so an edit rebuilds only its line, selection and search highlights as keyed rects, the cursor as a canvas reading the data each paint, folds as a region. Syntax highlighting per line is the cost to keep out of the per-keystroke path (cache by line text).\n\nSize: code/mod.rs is ~3,100 lines against the 1,000-line module limit. Split it along the seams as text_input and text_area were: data and editing, folding, highlighting and line rendering, handlers, the minimap, the read-only Code element.","files":null}]}